  "source_text": "Synthetic validation nurse note. No real patient data.\n\nThe driver reports a single home blood-pressure display of 179/103 after carrying equipment upstairs, but the cuff size is unknown and today's resting measurement has not been repeated; no clinician has established persistent hypertension. He recalls a normal value some months ago and asks which figure should be used, while the nurse records both accounts without selecting a persistent value or deriving a period of treatment control.\n\nAn unsigned hearing worksheet contains a number of 46 dB, but it does not identify the ear, the tested frequencies or whether a hearing aid was worn. The driver says he can usually follow conversation yet misses some radio messages in background noise, and a formal assessment is booked rather than completed.\n\nThe driver describes no witnessed loss of consciousness but sometimes feels light-headed when standing quickly. No current specialist conclusion explains the symptom. The note intentionally contains concerns worth retrieving guidance for, while lacking a verified persistent BP and a valid unaided better-ear average; neither numerical threshold should be treated as established by these fragments.\n",
